# Chain Fast Path

Purpose:
  Simplified execution path for common operations. Reduces
  overhead for simple tasks while maintaining safety.

## Fast Path Conditions

A task qualifies for fast path when:
  - Risk is LOW or MEDIUM
  - No architectural changes
  - No database schema changes
  - No security policy changes
  - No production deployment
  - Uses existing patterns
  - Dependencies are available

## Fast Path Stages

Simplified chain for qualifying tasks:

  D0
    ↓
  P1 (prompt) — analyze intent
    ↓
  D1 (docs) — generate minimal docs
    ↓
  S1 (sdd) — update project state
    ↓
  C1 (code) — implement
    ↓
  R1 (review) — automated review
    ↓
  D0

Skipped stages:
  - DEP1 (deploy) — human deploys manually
  - Optional QA — if tests pass automatically

## Fast Path Rules

  [FP1] Fast path ONLY for LOW/MEDIUM risk tasks
  [FP2] Fast path requires all dependencies available
  [FP3] Fast path skips human gates for non-critical items
  [FP4] Fast path still creates decisions for significant changes
  [FP5] Fast path logs all steps for audit
  [FP6] Fast path can be overridden by human
  [FP7] Fast path fails over to full chain if issues detected

## Fast Path Decision Tree

  Task Received
    ↓
  Risk Assessment
    ↓
  LOW/MEDIUM? ───NO───> Full Chain
    ↓ YES
  Architectural Change? ───YES───> Full Chain
    ↓ NO
  DB Schema Change? ───YES───> Full Chain
    ↓ NO
  Security Policy Change? ───YES───> Full Chain
    ↓ NO
  Production Deploy? ───YES───> Full Chain
    ↓ NO
  Dependencies Available? ───NO───> Full Chain
    ↓ YES
  Fast Path ← ← ← ← ← ← ← ← ← ← ←

## Fast Path vs Full Chain

| Aspect | Fast Path | Full Chain |
|--------|-----------|------------|
| Stages | 5 | 6+ |
| Human Gates | Minimal | All |
| Token Usage | ~30% | 100% |
| Speed | Fast | Thorough |
| Risk Coverage | Basic | Complete |
| Use Case | Simple tasks | Complex tasks |

## Fast Path Examples

Simple Bug Fix:
  P1 → D1 → S1 → C1 → R1 → D0
  Example: "Fix typo in error message"

Small Feature:
  P1 → D1 → S1 → C1 → R1 → D0
  Example: "Add logging to payment service"

Refactor:
  P1 → D1 → S1 → C1 → R1 → D0
  Example: "Extract common validation logic"

## Fast Path Safety

Safety Mechanisms:
  - Automated tests must pass
  - Lint must pass
  - No breaking changes
  - Backward compatible
  - Rollback available

Safety Checks:
  [FP-S1] Tests pass
  [FP-S2] Lint passes
  [FP-S3] No breaking API changes
  [FP-S4] Backward compatible
  [FP-S5] Rollback tested

Fast Path Failure:
  - If any safety check fails → Full Chain
  - If human overrides → Full Chain
  - If dependencies missing → Full Chain

## Fast Path Configuration

Default:
  enabled: true
  max_risk: MEDIUM
  require_tests: true
  require_lint: true
  auto_deploy: false

Per-Project:
  fast_path:
    enabled: <boolean>
    max_risk: <LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH>
    require_tests: <boolean>
    require_lint: <boolean>
    auto_deploy: <boolean>
    skip_stages: [<list>]

## Fast Path Metrics

Track:
  - Fast path usage rate
  - Fast path success rate
  - Fast path vs full chain comparison
  - Time saved
  - Token saved
  - Failure reasons

## Fast Path Commands

/sdd-fast:
  Execute task using fast path
  Usage: /sdd-fast "fix typo in error message"

/sdd-full:
  Force full chain execution
  Usage: /sdd-full "refactor payment module"

/sdd-auto:
  Auto-select path based on risk
  Usage: /sdd-auto "add logging"

State: +
